<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<assembly manifestVersion="1.0" xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1">
  <!--
    The CLI's Windows application manifest (branch EphePath, 1.7.2; finding N4). Ignored on macOS and Linux.

    WHY IT EXISTS: activeCodePage. The Swiss Ephemeris library takes its ephemeris folder as a C string
    (swe_set_ephe_path) and opens its files through its own C runtime, both in the process's ANSI code
    page. The folder is the program's own plus "ephe", so it holds whatever the user's profile or chosen
    folder is called. Without this entry a letter the system's code page cannot hold (a Polish, Greek or
    Japanese name on a Western system) was replaced on the way, the library looked in a folder that does
    not exist, and no chart could be cast. With UTF-8 as the process's code page every path survives, and
    the marshalling in SwissEphInterop carries it unchanged. Windows 10 version 1903 and later honour the
    entry; an earlier Windows ignores it and behaves as before.

    WHAT MUST STAY: the trustInfo block is the whole of the default manifest the build embedded until
    1.7.2 (asInvoker, no UI access); it is repeated here because a manifest of our own replaces the
    default one. Removing activeCodePage brings the fault back. No display-scaling (DPI) setting belongs
    here: that is finding N25.
  -->
  <assemblyIdentity version="1.0.0.0" name="AstroCast.Cli" />

  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v2">
    <security>
      <requestedPrivileges xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
        <requestedExecutionLevel level="asInvoker" uiAccess="false" />
      </requestedPrivileges>
    </security>
  </trustInfo>

  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <activeCodePage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2019/WindowsSettings">UTF-8</activeCodePage>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
</assembly>
